    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    margin: 12px 0;
  }
  .takeaway strong { color: var(--accent); }
  .limitation {
    background: #fdf2e9;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--warning);
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    margin: 14px 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
  .two-col {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 24px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .two-col { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .toc ul { columns: 1; }
    header h1 { font-size: 1.6rem; }
  }
  footer {
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    padding: 24px;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
<header>
  <h1>Biomedical Research Landscape 2024–2026</h1>
  <p>Publication Trends, Topic Dynamics, and Open Access Patterns across 4,886 PubMed-Indexed Abstracts</p>
  <div class="meta">Report generated: April 2026 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Audience: Research Policy Analysts &amp; Funding Strategists</div>
</header>

<div class="toc">
  <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#executive-summary">Executive Summary</a></li>
    <li><a href="#kpis">Key Performance Indicators</a></li>
    <li><a href="#publication-volume">Publication Volume &amp; Temporal Distribution</a></li>
    <li><a href="#geographic">Geographic Landscape</a></li>
    <li><a href="#topics">Research Topic &amp; MeSH Keyword Analysis</a></li>
    <li><a href="#open-access">Open Access Adoption &amp; Journal Landscape</a></li>
    <li><a href="#research-type">Research Type &amp; Collaboration</a></li>
    <li><a href="#takeaways">Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</a></li>
    <li><a href="#methodology">Methodology &amp; Data Quality Notes</a></li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section id="executive-summary">
  <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
  <p>This report examines <strong>4,886 biomedical research abstracts</strong> indexed in PubMed from January 2024 through March 2026. The dataset reveals a heavily front-loaded temporal profile—approximately 80% of records originate from 2024, reflecting the natural indexing lag inherent to PubMed. Despite this skew, several clear strategic signals emerge for policy analysts and funding strategists.</p>

  <p>China dominates the geographic landscape with nearly 23% of all publications, followed by the USA at 11%. However, India and Iran are gaining relative share in 2025, suggesting a gradual geographic diversification. Open access (OA) adoption stands at <strong>65.1%</strong> overall, with 2025 showing a markedly higher OA rate (74.1%) than 2024 (64.1%)—a trend that bears watching as 2026 data matures. COVID-19 remains the single most prevalent major MeSH topic, though its concentration is declining as newer publications diversify into microbiome research, oncology, and neurodegeneration.</p>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Headline insight:</strong> The combination of rising OA rates in 2025 and the emergence of India/Iran as growth markets suggests that funders should anticipate a more open, more geographically distributed research ecosystem—provided indexing lags do not obscure real-time signals.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="kpis">
  <h2>Key Performance Indicators</h2>
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">''' + f"{data['total_pubs']:,}" + '''</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Publications</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">2024: ''' + f"{data['total_2024']:,}" + ''' &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2025: ''' + f"{data['total_2025']:,}" + ''' &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2026: ''' + f"{data['total_2026']:,}" + '''</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">''' + f"{data['oa_rate']}%" + '''</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Open Access Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">2024: ''' + f"{data['oa_2024']}%" + ''' &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2025: ''' + f"{data['oa_2025']}%" + ''' &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2026: ''' + f"{data['oa_2026']}%" + '''</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">''' + f"{data['median_authors']}" + '''</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Median Authors / Paper</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Proxy for collaboration intensity</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">''' + f"{data['top10_share']}%" + '''</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Top 10 Countries Share</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Of total dataset</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="publication-volume">
  <h2>Publication Volume &amp; Temporal Distribution</h2>
  <p>The temporal distribution of the dataset is heavily skewed toward 2024, which accounts for <strong>''' + f"{data['total_2024']:,}" + ''' publications (80.3% of the total)</strong>. This is not a data error but a well-documented artifact of PubMed indexing: older publications accumulate more complete metadata over time, while very recent papers may still be awaiting full indexing. 2025 contributes ''' + f"{data['total_2025']:,}" + ''' records (15.1%) and 2026 contributes ''' + f"{data['total_2026']:,}" + ''' (4.6%) through March.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="monthlyVolumeChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <p>Monthly patterns within 2024 show a pronounced January spike (1,665 records), followed by a steep decline through the spring and summer. This likely reflects batch indexing of backlogged 2023–2024 content rather than a true seasonal surge in publishing. By contrast, 2025 exhibits a more even monthly distribution from February onward, with a modest uptick in December (94 records). The first quarter of 2026 shows accelerating counts (51 → 76 → 98), suggesting that indexing is catching up with the publication pipeline.</p>

  <div class="limitation">
    <strong>Data limitation:</strong> 754 records (15.4%) lack precise month-level metadata and are recorded as "Unknown." These are concentrated in 2024 (575) and 2025 (179). Monthly trend analyses should be interpreted with this gap in mind.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="geographic">
  <h2>Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>
  <p>The top 10 contributing countries account for <strong>''' + f"{data['top10_share']}%" + '''</strong> of all indexed abstracts. China leads decisively with 1,121 records (22.9%), roughly double the USA’s 539 (11.0%). India (4.0%), Japan (2.8%), and Canada (2.2%) round out the top five.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Top 10 Countries by Volume</h3>
      <div class="chart-container small">
        <canvas id="countryChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Country Share Shift (2024 → 2025)</h3>
      <div class="chart-container small">
        <canvas id="countryShiftChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Country</th><th class="text-right">Total</th><th class="text-right">Share</th><th class="text-right">OA Rate</th><th class="text-right">Median Authors</th><th class="text-right">2024</th><th class="text-right">2025</th><th class="text-right">2026</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for c in data['country_data']:
    html += f'''      <tr><td>{c['country']}</td><td class="text-right">{c['count']:,}</td><td class="text-right">{c['share']}%</td><td class="text-right">{c['oa_rate']}%</td><td class="text-right">{c['median_authors']}</td><td class="text-right">{c['count_2024']:,}</td><td class="text-right">{c['count_2025']:,}</td><td class="text-right">{c['count_2026']:,}</td></tr>
'''

html += '''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Strategic signal:</strong> India’s share jumped from 3.3% in 2024 to 8.0% in 2025 (+4.7 percentage points), while Iran rose from 2.0% to 3.7%. Conversely, China’s share fell from 24.8% to 13.8%. These shifts may reflect either genuine changes in research output or differential indexing speeds across national repositories.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="topics">
  <h2>Research Topic &amp; MeSH Keyword Analysis</h2>
  <p>MeSH indexing is incomplete for recent publications: ''' + f"{data['missing_mesh']:,}" + ''' records (''' + f"{data['missing_mesh_pct']}%" + ''') lack major topic or keyword assignments. This is expected for 2025–2026 papers and should not be interpreted as a data quality failure. Among indexed topics, <strong>COVID-19</strong> remains the dominant major topic (43 total), though its share is concentrated in 2024 (37 of 43).</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Top 15 Major MeSH Topics</h3>
      <div class="chart-container small">
        <canvas id="topicChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Top 15 MeSH Keywords</h3>
      <div class="chart-container small">
        <canvas id="keywordChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Major Topic</th><th class="text-right">Total</th><th class="text-right">2024</th><th class="text-right">2025</th><th class="text-right">2026</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for t in data['mt_data']:
    html += f'''      <tr><td>{t['topic']}</td><td class="text-right">{t['count']}</td><td class="text-right">{t['count_2024']}</td><td class="text-right">{t['count_2025']}</td><td class="text-right">{t['count_2026']}</td></tr>
'''

html += '''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Emerging topic signal:</strong> While most top topics are dominated by 2024 records, <em>Coleoptera</em> (entomology/biodiversity) shows a rising 2025 presence (3 of 10 total). New topics appearing in 2025–2026 include <em>Extracellular Vesicles</em>, <em>Catheterization, Peripheral</em>, and <em>Anthropometry</em>—each with 2 records. These are early signals rather than established trends, but they hint at diversification beyond infectious disease and oncology.
  </div>

  <p>Among MeSH keywords, <em>Humans</em> (1,842), <em>Female</em> (707), and <em>Male</em> (606) dominate—as expected for clinical and population-health research. <em>Animals</em> (537) and <em>Mice</em> (170) reflect strong preclinical activity. Notably, <em>COVID-19</em> appears as a keyword in only 88 records, suggesting that pandemic-related research is increasingly framed through other disease lenses (e.g., respiratory aerosols, vaccines) rather than the explicit COVID-19 descriptor.</p>
</section>

<section id="open-access">
  <h2>Open Access Adoption &amp; Journal Landscape</h2>
  <p>Overall, <strong>''' + f"{data['oa_rate']}%" + '''</strong> of abstracts in the dataset are flagged as open access (available via PubMed Central). The year-over-year trajectory is non-linear: 64.1% in 2024, 74.1% in 2025, and 53.8% in 2026 (partial, through March). The 2026 dip is likely an indexing artifact—recent papers often enter PubMed before PMC archiving is complete.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="oaTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <p>Monthly OA rates fluctuate considerably, ranging from a low of 23.5% (March 2026) to a high of 88.2% (January 2026). The March 2026 outlier is driven by a single high-volume, low-OA journal batch and should not be over-interpreted. Excluding March 2026, the 2025–2026 monthly average sits at approximately 70%, reinforcing the impression of a structurally higher OA baseline than 2024.</p>

  <h3>OA Rate by Country</h3>
  <p>Open access penetration varies sharply by country. Iran leads the top 10 at 86.9%, followed by the UK (80.8%), Japan (77.4%), and the USA (77.0%). China trails at 54.7%, suggesting that Chinese publishing channels may have different OA policies or that Chinese authors favor subscription journals in the indexed sample.</p>

  <div class="chart-container small">
    <canvas id="oaCountryChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Leading Journals</h3>
  <p>The journal landscape is highly concentrated. The top 10 journals publish 431 records (8.8% of the dataset). Fully OA titles—<em>Scientific Reports</em>, <em>Heliyon</em>, <em>Cureus</em>, <em>PLOS ONE</em>, <em>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</em>, and <em>bioRxiv</em>—show 100% OA rates. By contrast, <em>The Science of the Total Environment</em> and <em>Zootaxa</em> have near-zero OA in this sample, reflecting hybrid or subscription models.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Journal</th><th class="text-right">Records</th><th class="text-right">OA Rate</th><th class="text-right">Model</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for j in data['journal_data']:
    model = 'Fully OA' if j['oa_rate'] >= 95 else 'Hybrid' if j['oa_rate'] >= 10 else 'Subscription'
    badge = 'badge-success' if model == 'Fully OA' else 'badge-info' if model == 'Hybrid' else 'badge-danger'
    html += f'''      <tr><td>{j['journal']}</td><td class="text-right">{j['count']}</td><td class="text-right">{j['oa_rate']}%</td><td class="text-center"><span class="badge {badge}">{model}</span></td></tr>
'''

html += '''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>OA by Research Type</h3>
  <p>Case reports show the highest OA rate (80.4%), possibly because they are shorter and cheaper to publish in OA venues. Journal articles and reviews cluster around 64–65%, while systematic reviews are lower at 57.1%—a counter-intuitive finding that may reflect the preference of high-impact subscription journals for synthesis papers.</p>
</section>

<section id="research-type">
  <h2>Research Type Distribution &amp; Collaboration Indicators</h2>
  <p>Journal articles dominate the corpus at 85.3% (4,169 records). Reviews comprise 8.4% (408), case reports 4.2% (204), and systematic reviews 1.7% (84). A small preprint contingent (41 records, 0.8%) appears as dual-classified "Preprint; Journal Article" entries, reflecting bioRxiv/medRxiv deposits that have subsequently been journal-published.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Research Type Distribution</h3>
      <div class="chart-container small">
        <canvas id="researchTypeChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Author Count Distribution</h3>
      <div class="chart-container small">
        <canvas id="authorDistChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Research Type</th><th class="text-right">Count</th><th class="text-right">Share</th><th class="text-right">OA Rate</th><th class="text-right">Median Authors</th><th class="text-right">Mean Authors</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for rt in data['rt_data']:
    html += f'''      <tr><td>{rt['type']}</td><td class="text-right">{rt['count']:,}</td><td class="text-right">{rt['share']}%</td><td class="text-right">{rt['oa_rate']}%</td><td class="text-right">{rt['median_authors']}</td><td class="text-right">{rt['mean_authors']}</td></tr>
'''

html += '''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Collaboration intensity, proxied by median author count, is relatively uniform across types: journal articles and systematic reviews both median at 6 authors, while reviews and case reports median at 5. The mean is pulled upward by large consortia—one paper lists 115 authors. China shows the highest median (7) among major countries, suggesting either larger research groups or more inclusive authorship practices.</p>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Collaboration pattern:</strong> The modal author count is 5 (642 papers), with a steep decline beyond 10 authors. This implies that most biomedical research in the sample is conducted by small-to-medium teams rather than massive international consortia.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="takeaways">
  <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <strong>1. Indexing lag is the dominant temporal artifact—plan for it.</strong><br>
    With 80% of records from 2024 and only 4.6% from 2026 (through March), any real-time policy analysis must apply significant discount factors to recent months. Funders should not interpret low 2026 volumes as a research slowdown.
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <strong>2. Open access is trending upward, but not uniformly.</strong><br>
